  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108628",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-131171",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Two Dimensions",
      "problem": "Given that $F=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,i+xy\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1+xy}{x}",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2x",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x(y+1)",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{1}{xy}",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source_answer_policy": {
