  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108628",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-131179",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e6b79409-5a5b-533b-8d46-4e0e6d963b3a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Divergence of a Vector Field in Three Dimensions",
      "problem": "Consider the vector field $F=x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+xy{e}^{z}\\,j+z(x+1)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,1,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z(x+1)) \\\\  & =(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+x{e}^{z}+(x+1) \\\\  & =y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x{e}^{z}+x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{0}+1+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1+1+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
