  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108829",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-126537",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field",
      "problem": "Given that $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(2x+3y)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+3y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =2-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
